<%# Was this session the thing your watch recorded? #520.

    On the record and not on the session screen, which is what #520 settled: the session
    screen is for lifting, and by the time there is anything to match the lifting is done.
    It also means the question survives a lifter who taps finish and puts the phone away --
    it is on a page they come back to rather than a moment they have to catch.

    **Four states, and telling them apart is the substance of the ticket.** A page with two
    -- match or nothing -- would say "no activity" about an upload that lands in ninety
    seconds and about a morning whose activities were all something else, and neither is a
    claim the app can support. They are `:matched`, `:proposed`, `:waiting` and `:elsewhere`;
    the last two were one state until #560, and the pair of them is the difference between
    "nothing has arrived" and "something arrived and it was not this".

    A fifth, `:unreachable`, used to be here and is no longer a state of the page, because
    the page no longer fetches: since #560 it reads stored rows and calls nobody, and a
    request is made only when a lifter presses check. A press Withings never answered is
    reported as a line inside the box below rather than as a state of the session, which is
    the honest place for it -- the failure is the press's, not the session's. The trap that
    state existed for is unchanged and is guarded in lib/tectonic/withings_workouts.rb:
    `answered` folds Withings' 601 rate limit into the same nil as a revoked token, so a
    throttled press and an empty one are told apart there or not at all.

    Nothing at all where there is nothing to ask. A session that was never matched and has
    been dismissed, one on an account with no Withings connection, and one old enough that
    nothing is coming and nothing overlaps it all render no markup whatever -- which is the
    property that keeps the prompt worth reading on the session where it does appear. %>
<%- if @withings && @withings[:state] == :proposed %>
  <%# The proposal. A question with the evidence attached, not an announcement.

      The sentence is the whole of why this is confirmable. A lifter cannot agree or
      disagree with a score of 1.05; they can agree with "overlaps this session for 48m of
      its 52m", which is the same fact said in a way that can be checked against a memory of
      the afternoon. #520 is explicit that an overlap in time is good evidence and not proof
      -- a dog walk at 18:00 and a lift at 18:30 both touch the window, and the watch may
      have called either one strength training -- so the evidence is shown and the judgement
      is the lifter's.

      Yes is the lime fill the app uses for work that was done; no is the plain bordered
      button, because refusing is not a lesser version of confirming, it is the other
      answer, and a greyed-out-looking control would read as the one you are not meant to
      press. %>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-gray-300 bg-amber-50 px-4 py-3">
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-900">
      Withings recorded an activity
      <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @withings[:activity][:started_at] }) %>
      to
      <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @withings[:activity][:ended_at] }) %>.
      Was that this session?
    </p>
    <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-600">It <%= @withings[:because] %>.</p>
    <%# That this is one of many, said on the side that could not otherwise know. #534.

        A backfill can leave a hundred of these at once, and nothing on a record page ever
        admitted it -- so the honest reading of this box, for a lifter who had not run the
        task or had forgotten they did, was that one stray Tuesday had a question on it. They
        would answer it, and the other ninety-nine would sit there being answered at whatever
        rate they happened to open old sessions.

        It says "other" and links rather than redirecting anybody, because the two doors are
        genuinely equal: this one is the right door when a lifter is already reading this
        session and can remember the afternoon, and the list is the right door for a sitting.
        The answer is the same write either way -- both forms below post to the routes the
        list posts to -- so there is nothing to lose by taking the question here and no reason
        to push somebody away from it.

        Nothing at all where this is the only one left, which is the ordinary state of the
        forward flow: a session finished ten minutes ago is asked about on its own record,
        there is no queue behind it, and a line saying "0 others" would invent one. %>
    <%- if questions_elsewhere.positive? %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600">
        <%= questions_elsewhere %> other session<%= 's' unless questions_elsewhere == 1 %>
        <%= questions_elsewhere == 1 ? 'is' : 'are' %> waiting on the same question.
        <a href="/workouts/withings" class="underline">Answer them together</a>.
      </p>
    <%- end %>
    <div class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-2">
      <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/withings/match">
        <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/withings/match") %>
        <input type="hidden" name="activity" value="<%= @withings[:activity][:external_id] %>">
        <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 bg-lime-500 px-3 text-sm font-semibold
                                      text-gray-900 hover:bg-lime-600">Yes, that was this session</button>
      </form>
      <%# Dismissing carries the activity it was shown with, so "no" can be recorded about
          both things at once: this session is answered, and this activity is not a lift the
          lifter logged. Without the id the second half would be guessed. %>
      <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/withings/dismiss">
        <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/withings/dismiss") %>
        <input type="hidden" name="activity" value="<%= @withings[:activity][:external_id] %>">
        <button type="submit" class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3
                                      text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">No</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
<%- elsif @withings && %i[waiting elsewhere].include?(@withings[:state]) %>
  <%# Nothing to propose, said in whichever of the two ways is true. #560.

      **These were one state and one sentence**, and the sentence was *"Nothing from Withings
      yet -- check back in a minute."* It fits the first case and lies about the second. On
      the reporting account the watch had recorded two activities that morning and neither
      overlapped the session being read; the page said nothing had arrived, about two things
      that had, and advised waiting for a third that was never coming. Telling them apart
      costs one query -- see `nearby` -- and it matters more now than it did, because a
      lifter who has just pressed check is owed an answer rather than a hedge.

      **And `:elsewhere` names them, which is #568.** *"Withings has 2 activities around this
      session, and none of them overlaps it"* was true and still not enough to act on: it
      states a number and leaves the lifter unable to check it, to recognise their own
      afternoon in it, or to notice that one of the two is plainly the lift they are reading,
      filed against a clock that disagrees. The page had every one of those answers in hand --
      `nearby` selected the rows and kept the count -- and said none of them.

      **The session's own two ends are half of it.** Two clock times for an activity are a
      fact about the watch; the same two beside the session's own are the evidence for the
      sentence above them, and a reader can see for themselves that 13:13 to 14:18 is not
      13:10 to 13:48. Asserting a mismatch while withholding the numbers is asking to be taken
      on trust about the one thing that is cheap to show. They come out of the hash rather
      than off `@timing` so that what is printed is the interval the comparison was made
      against.

      **A cap rather than everything**, at `NAMED`, because this is a box under a session on a
      phone, and a lifter whose watch recorded ten things that day is not served by ten lines
      pushing the controls below the fold. The three nearest the session and a count of the
      rest says what was recorded and admits there is more; a wall would be a second way of
      being unreadable.

      Still `:elsewhere` and still not `:waiting`. What #560 split is the claim -- *"nothing
      has arrived"* against *"something arrived and it was not this"* -- and naming what
      arrived is the second claim made properly, not the two folded back together. The other
      branch is untouched and keeps its wording exactly.

      **Late is not absent**, which is #520's rule and survives intact in the first branch.
      Closing a workout in the Withings app starts an upload; their servers get it seconds to
      minutes later, so a lifter who taps finish and reads this page at once is in the
      ordinary case and "no activity found" would be a lie about a thing on its way. It is
      the same distinction 041 draws by storing `measured_at` apart from when a row appeared.
      Neither branch appears at all on a session old enough that no upload can be coming --
      `propose` answers nothing there, and nothing is the honest rendering of absence.

      **Check is the prominent control and dismiss is no longer a button-shaped one.** They
      used to be one control, dismiss, which is the irreversible one: the box asked somebody
      to do a thing and then offered them only the way to silence it. Check reads as the
      action because it is the reversible action; dismiss is still plainly there, and still
      posts no activity id, because there is no activity and the refusal is about the
      session. That is what keeps #520's "do not nag" honest for the commonest case of all,
      a lifter who simply did not wear the watch for this one. %>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-md border border-gray-300 bg-white px-4 py-3">
    <%- if @withings[:state] == :elsewhere %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">
        <%- if @withings[:count] == 1 %>
          Withings has one activity around this session, and it does not overlap it:
        <%- else %>
          Withings has <%= @withings[:count] %> activities around this session, and none of
          them overlaps it:
        <%- end %>
      </p>
      <%# What the watch actually recorded, named and timed. #568.

          The category first and in the darker grey, because it is the word a lifter reads to
          decide whether any of this is theirs -- "Lift weights" on a day they did not think
          they had lifted is the whole reason to look further, and "Bicycling" ends the
          question in one glance. It is `called`, so a category Withings has published no name
          for prints as its id rather than as an invented word; see the note there.

          The day is said only where it differs from the session's, and said against the
          session rather than against today -- "the day before", never "yesterday", which
          would be a claim about this morning on a record from March.

          `_clock_time` for both ends, exactly as the proposal branch above does it: the
          server has no idea what timezone anybody is in (#349), so the instant goes over the
          wire and the reader's own browser formats it. Formatting these by hand here would
          be the one place on the page that tells a lifter in California their afternoon
          happened at one in the morning. %>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-sm text-gray-700">
        <%- @withings[:nearby].each do |activity| %>
          <li>
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= Tectonic::WithingsActivity.called(activity[:category]) %></span>
            &mdash;
            <%- if (day = Tectonic::WithingsWorkouts.day_apart(activity[:started_at], @withings[:session])) %>
              <%= day %>,
            <%- end %>
            <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: activity[:started_at] }) %>
            to
            <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: activity[:ended_at] }) %>
          </li>
        <%- end %>
        <%# The ones there was no room for, counted rather than dropped in silence. A box
            that showed three of eleven and said so is telling the truth about a busy day;
            one that showed three and stopped would be the same withholding in a smaller
            font. %>
        <%- if @withings[:count] > @withings[:nearby].length %>
          <li>and <%= @withings[:count] - @withings[:nearby].length %> more around this session</li>
        <%- end %>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-700">
        This session ran
        <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @withings[:session].first }) %>
        to
        <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @withings[:session].last }) %>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-600">
        So something was recorded and it was not this session. Checking again will only help
        if your watch has not finished uploading this one.
      </p>
    <%- else %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-700">Nothing from Withings for this session yet.</p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-600">
        Closing a workout in the Withings app starts an upload that reaches their servers a
        minute or two later, so it may still be on its way.
      </p>
    <%- end %>
    <%# What the press concluded, which is the one thing the stored rows cannot say.

        A fetch Withings never answered writes nothing, so without this line the page after
        a press would be byte for byte the page before it -- somebody asking a question and
        being handed back the same screen. The throttle is the case that makes it necessary:
        Withings delivers a rate limit as body status 601 over HTTP 200, indistinguishable
        from a revoked token by the time it reaches the app, so "could not be reached" is the
        strongest honest thing there is to say and "nothing arrived" would be a claim about
        an afternoon nobody managed to ask about.

        There is a dismiss button under this, where the old unreachable state had none on the
        grounds that dismissing answers a question the app had failed to ask. The reasoning
        does not carry over: this is no longer a box about a failed fetch, it is the box
        about the session with a note about a press on it, and a lifter is entitled to say
        "stop asking about this one" whether or not Withings answered. %>
    <%- if @withings_checked == 'answered' %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600">Checked just now.</p>
    <%- elsif @withings_checked == 'unreachable' %>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-600">
        Withings could not be reached just now, so none of this has changed.
      </p>
    <%- end %>
    <div class="mt-3 flex items-center gap-3">
      <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/withings/check">
        <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/withings/check") %>
        <button type="submit"
                class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 border border-gray-300 bg-white px-3 text-sm
                       font-medium text-gray-700 hover:bg-gray-50">Check Withings now</button>
      </form>
      <form method="post" action="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/withings/dismiss">
        <%== csrf_tag("/workouts/#{@workout[:id]}/withings/dismiss") %>
        <button type="submit"
                class="<%= button_style %> min-h-11 px-1 text-sm text-gray-600 underline
                       hover:text-gray-900">Don&rsquo;t ask about this session</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
<%- elsif matched_activity %>
  <%# Matched: everything a match is now worth. #571.

      This block used to be the leftovers. The length and the two ends sat on the lines above
      taken from the watch and labelled as its, per #520's rule that the watch is right where
      the two disagree, so what was left down here was a heart rate and a calorie count.
      #571 reversed that rule on the reporting account's own evidence -- their watch starts
      late and runs long, calling a 46 minute session an hour -- and the session's length and
      its two ends are the taps again. Which makes this block the whole of what answering
      "yes, that was this session" now buys.

      It is still plainly worth buying, and that is worth saying rather than assuming.
      Nothing in this app can know that a session sat at 128 bpm and touched 164. There is no
      arithmetic over taps, weights or reps that produces it, no second source to derive it
      from, and it is the one number here that describes what the training cost the lifter
      rather than what they did. A match is how it gets onto the page.

      **The heart rate says three figures now where it said two.** The low was stored on
      every fetch since 042 and shown nowhere, and an average without its range cannot tell
      intervals from a grind -- see `watch_heart_rate` in app.rb, which does the choosing,
      including the part about a figure being absent rather than zero where the watch's
      sensor recorded nothing.

      **The watch's own recording keeps a line, deliberately down here.** It is real, it is
      stored, and it is the interval the overlap was computed from, so a lifter looking at
      their watch saying an hour is owed the reconciliation rather than left wondering where
      the number went -- and the gap between the two lines is now legible instead of hidden,
      which on this data is information about the watch. What it must never be again is the
      figure labelled as the session, so it is named as the watch's recording, kept out of
      the block above, and given its span in brackets rather than as a headline.

      Named as the watch's throughout -- "your watch", not "measured" on its own -- because
      the whole of the page's honesty here is that a reader can always tell which instrument
      a number came from. That was #528's principle and #571 does not touch it; what changed
      is which instrument gets to answer "how long did this take". %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
    Matched to your watch
    <%- if watch_heart_rate %>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <%= watch_heart_rate %>
    <%- end %>
    <%- if matched_activity[:calories] %>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <%= matched_activity[:calories].to_i %> kcal
    <%- end %>
  </p>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
    Your watch&rsquo;s own recording ran
    <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: matched_activity[:started_at] }) %>
    to
    <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: matched_activity[:ended_at] }) %>
    (<%= Timing.phrase(watch_seconds) %>).
  </p>
<%- end %>
